// A quantifier inside a SAPIC conditional, over a bound and a free variable
// of the same name.
//
// `quantify` closes a free variable into `Bound i` by comparing the whole
// variable (Theory/Model/Formula.hs:346-352#quantify), and the printer opens
// it again with a name `freshLVar` picks against the formula's own supply
// (Theory/Model/Formula.hs:274-291#openFormula, reached from
// Theory/Model/Formula.hs:474-514#prettyLFormula and seeded by `avoidPrecise`
// at Theory/Model/Formula.hs:523-525#prettySyntacticLNFormula).  So the two
// branches print as
//
//   if (∃ y. Eq( y, x.1 )) ∧ (Eq( x.1, 'a' ))
//   if (∃ x.2. Eq( x.2, 'b' )) ∧ (Eq( x.1, 'a' ))
//
// — the first binder keeps its written name beside the free `x.1` of the
// `in`, and the second binder, written with the free variable's own name, is
// opened as `x.2` and cannot collide with it.  The rule names derived from
// those strings (Sapic/Facts.hs:401#stripNonAlphanumerical) carry the binder
// names as well: `ifyEqyxEqxa_0_11` and `ifxEqxbEqxa_0_12`.
//
// Mode `match`: the two engines agree on this shape, and the row exists so
// that they keep agreeing.  Corpus conditions are quantifier-free, so no gate
// row covers a bound occurrence.
//
// Compared slices: the wf report block, which pins the run clean, AND the
// `theory … end` echo, which carries the `process=` attribute of each of the
// four conditional rules and the names derived from it.

theory SapicCondBinder
begin

predicates: Eq(a, b) <=> a = b

process:
  in(x);
  ( ( if (Ex y. Eq(y, x)) & Eq(x, 'a') then out('yes') else out('no') )
  | ( if (Ex x. Eq(x, 'b')) & Eq(x, 'a') then out('shadow') else out('free') ) )

end
